WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Good morning, Hank.  It's the day after you got featured on YouTube, good work!

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.280
In honor of the recent video you made,  where you told those 50 jokes in 4 minutes,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.580
And also in honor of my first book, "Looking For Alaska"

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.100
which is about a kid who memorizes last words of famous people,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.860
today I am going to share with you the real last words of 50 famous and deceased individuals.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:23.860
And...go!

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.840
George Washington's last word were, "'Tis well."

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.060
John Adams said, "Thomas Jefferson still survives," which, curiously, wasn't true.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.260
Jefferson had died earlier that same day, July 4th, 1826.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.180
Jefferson's last words were, "Is it the fourth?"

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.780
When Henry Gibson's nurse said "You seem to be feeling much better today,"

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.700
He said, "On the contrary."

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.280
Winston Churchill said, "I'm bored with it all."

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.320
The Roman emperor Vespasian said, "Woe is me, I think I'm becoming a God."

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.780
Nero said, "What an artist the world is losing in me."

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.280
Those Roman emperors sure knew a thing or two about humility.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:55.320
When Lady Astro woke up from a stupor to find everyone she knew in her room, she said, "Am I dying or is this my birthday?"

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.840
It wasn't her birthday.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.600
The poet Dylan Thomas's last known words were, "I've had eighteen straight whiskies, I believe that's the record."

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.800
Abraham Lincoln said to his wife, "they won't think anything of it."

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.320
Just before John Wilkes Booth shot him.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.480
Booth's last words were, "Useless, useless."

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.120
Henry Ward Beacher: "Now comes the mystery."

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.500
The Irish playright, Brendan Behan turned to a nun, who was drawing his blood and said

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.720
"Bless you sister, may all your sons be bishops."

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.900
Che Guevara "Shoot coward! You are only going to kill a man!"

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.160
Pancho Villa wasn't quite so composed, his last words were:

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.420
"Don't let it end like this! Tell them I said something!"

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.060
Ben Franklin "A dying man can do nothing easy."

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.380
President Millard Fillmore after a sip of porridge said: "The nourishment is palatable."

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.400
F.D.R : "I have a terrific headache."

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.360
Robert E. Lee : "Strike the tent!"

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.480
Civil War Union General John Sedgwick was staring across the Confederate lines

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.460
and he said : "They couldn't hit an elephant at this dis-".

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.040
Confederate General Stonewall Jackson said

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.460
"Let us cross over the river and rest under the shade of those trees."

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.680
Charles Darwin said : "I am not in the least afraid to die."

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.260
The enlightenment writer Voltaire, when asked on his deathbed to repudiate the Devil, said:

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.700
"This doesn't seem like a good time to be making enemies."

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.600
Eugene O'Neil said : "I knew it! Born in a hotel room, and dammit, dying in a hotel room."

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.720
Elizabeth Barret Browning when asked how she was feeling said : "Beautiful."

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.760
Emily Dickinson,  said: "I must go in, the fog is rising."

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.980
The writer Paul Claudel wasn't quite so symbolically resonant, his last words were "Doctor, do you think it was the sausage?"

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:14.250
O'Henry: "Turn up the lights, I don't want to go home in the dark."

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.160
Oscar Wilde, dying in a garishly decorated hotel room, famously said, "Either that wallpaper

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.340
goes,

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:23.180
or I do." Tolstoy said, "The truth is I care a great deal how they-" and then died

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.560
before finishing the sentence.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.940
The writer Lope de Vega, told he was about to die, said: "Alright then, I'll say it.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:28.319
 

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:31.670
Dante makes me sick." When John Field was dying, he was asked whether he was

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.220
a Papist or a Calvinist, and he answered

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:37.239
"I'm a pianist." Queen Elizabeth? "All of my
possesions for a moment in time."

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.860
W.C Fieldsman, and I'm gonna tone this down for any little nerd fighter here, said: "Gosh darn it, the whole frackin world and everyone in it but you Carlatta."

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.700
Which sounds romantic until you learn that his wife was named Hattie. Carlatta was his mistress.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.489
Frans Kafka, begging for a morphine overdose, said, "Kill me, or else you are a murderer."

00:02:51.489 --> 00:02:54.620
After a doctor told him that the angels
were waiting for him, General Ethan Allen said,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.610
"Waiting are they?

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.050
Well let 'em wait!"  Then he died.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.510
Peter Pan author, J.M. Barrie said, "I can't sleep."

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.590
Yes, you can.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.590
Pablo Picasso?  "Drink to me!"

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.160
Malcolm X?  "This is a house of peace."

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:11.240
When he was asked if he had any parting words of wisdom the hotelier Conrad Hilton said, "Leave the shower curtain on the inside of the tub."

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:16.340
Which is good advice, although, in retrospect, I would have preferred it if he'd said, "Never let my granddaughter, Paris, in front of a video camera."

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.750
The economist John Maynard Keenan said, "I wish I'd drunk more champagne."

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.750
The famous Australian outlaw, Net Kelly, pulled a Vonnegut and said, "So it goes."

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.090
Edgar Allan Poe: "Lord, help my poor soul."

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.890
Second City's Del Close said,

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:29.740
"I'm tired of being the funniest person in the room."

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.470
Walt Whitman?  "Shift, shift!"

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.940
Thomas Edison said, "It's very beautiful over there."

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.980
George Bernard Shaw?  "Well, it'll be a new experience, anyway."

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.180
HG Wells?  "Go away, I'm alright."

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.100
Daniel Webster?  "I still live."

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.659
And finally, Beethoven:

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:45.299
"Friends, applaud.  The comedy is over."

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.440
Hank!  I'll see you tomorrow!

